FINAL IVX IA INTELLIGENCE UPGRADE
MAKE IVX IA REASON, REMEMBER, SEARCH, PLAN, EXECUTE, VERIFY, AND COMMUNICATE LIKE A HIGH-QUALITY SENIOR AI ASSISTANT

EXECUTE IN PHASES.
DO NOT REPLACE THE CURRENT WORKING EXECUTION ENGINE.
EXTEND IT WITH A REAL INTELLIGENCE LAYER.

The objective is not to add more buttons or more narrative.

The objective is to create one owner-facing IVX IA that can:

- understand the owner’s real intent;
- retrieve the correct company context;
- remember durable decisions;
- reason before acting;
- select the correct tools;
- execute through specialized workers;
- verify independently;
- respond clearly with evidence;
- learn from completed work.

==================================================
PHASE 1 — MODEL GATEWAY
==================================================

Create one provider-independent model gateway.

Required capabilities:

- reasoning model selection;
- fast model selection;
- vision model selection;
- embedding model selection;
- fallback provider;
- timeout;
- retries;
- token limits;
- cost limits;
- structured JSON output;
- streaming;
- provider health;
- per-task model policy.

Required model selection rules:

- simple question → fast model;
- architecture/debugging → reasoning model;
- screenshot/file analysis → vision-capable model;
- retrieval → embeddings + reranking;
- code verification → reasoning model plus tools;
- final owner summary → high-quality response model.

Do not hardcode one model throughout the system.

Return:

MODEL_GATEWAY_FILE:
PROVIDERS:
ROUTING_POLICY:
FALLBACK_POLICY:
COST_POLICY:
TESTS:

==================================================
PHASE 2 — CONTEXT ENGINE
==================================================

Before every answer, construct a structured context package.

Required context sources:

- authenticated user;
- owner/member role;
- current conversation;
- conversation summary;
- current screen;
- selected entity;
- recent tasks;
- current GitHub SHA;
- current Render runtime SHA;
- production health;
- relevant Supabase records;
- uploaded files;
- recent errors;
- open blockers;
- approval status;
- long-term memory;
- retrieved documentation.

Required context structure:

{
  "user": {},
  "screen": {},
  "conversation": {},
  "entities": [],
  "memory": [],
  "retrievedSources": [],
  "production": {},
  "permissions": {},
  "openTasks": [],
  "uncertainties": []
}

Implement token budgeting so irrelevant history is not sent to the model.

Required tests:

- current deal question retrieves the correct deal;
- registration question retrieves auth and registration context;
- code question retrieves repository and runtime context;
- follow-up “fix this” resolves the previous referenced issue;
- unrelated old history is excluded.

==================================================
PHASE 3 — MEMORY SYSTEM
==================================================

Implement three memory classes:

1. SESSION MEMORY
   - current conversation;
   - temporary choices;
   - current task;
   - temporary files.

2. USER MEMORY
   - owner preferences;
   - communication style;
   - recurring requirements;
   - approval preferences.

3. COMPANY MEMORY
   - architecture decisions;
   - canonical repositories;
   - infrastructure;
   - business rules;
   - brand rules;
   - previous verified fixes;
   - known incidents.

Every memory record must include:

- memory ID;
- category;
- source;
- confidence;
- verified status;
- created timestamp;
- updated timestamp;
- expiration policy;
- superseded-by reference;
- owner visibility;
- deletion support.

The AI must not treat an assumption as verified memory.

Add owner controls:

- show memory;
- correct memory;
- delete memory;
- mark permanent;
- mark temporary.

==================================================
PHASE 4 — ENTERPRISE RETRIEVAL
==================================================

Create a retrieval system across:

- GitHub code;
- GitHub issues and commits;
- Render logs;
- Supabase schemas and records;
- IVX documentation;
- owner chat history;
- task proof ledger;
- investor records;
- buyer records;
- properties;
- deals;
- messages;
- media;
- reports;
- uploaded documents.

Required retrieval pipeline:

QUERY UNDERSTANDING
→ SOURCE SELECTION
→ SEARCH
→ FILTER BY PERMISSIONS
→ RERANK
→ DEDUPLICATE
→ CONTEXT COMPRESSION
→ SOURCE CITATION

Every factual answer must identify its source when retrieval was used.

Add freshness rules:

- production/runtime facts must be checked live;
- business rules may use verified memory;
- stale records must be marked stale;
- conflicting sources must be shown to the verifier.

==================================================
PHASE 5 — INTENT AND COMPLEXITY CLASSIFIER
==================================================

Classify each owner request by:

- informational question;
- analysis;
- planning;
- code inspection;
- QA;
- bug fix;
- feature;
- module;
- new app;
- database;
- deployment;
- destructive action;
- business analysis;
- investor workflow;
- content generation;
- visual analysis.

Also classify complexity:

- simple;
- moderate;
- complex;
- critical.

The classification must determine:

- model;
- context depth;
- tools;
- specialist agents;
- approval requirements;
- verification requirements;
- response format.

==================================================
PHASE 6 — INTERNAL PLANNER
==================================================

For moderate, complex, or critical tasks, generate an internal plan containing:

- objective;
- acceptance criteria;
- known facts;
- unknown facts;
- assumptions;
- dependencies;
- relevant files;
- relevant infrastructure;
- task graph;
- tool sequence;
- risk;
- rollback strategy;
- verification strategy.

The plan must be saved with the task but should not create excessive owner-facing narrative.

The owner should see:

- objective;
- current stage;
- major actions;
- blocker;
- evidence.

==================================================
PHASE 7 — SPECIALIST AGENT ROUTING
==================================================

Create internal specialists:

1. IVX Architect
2. IVX Senior Developer
3. IVX Mobile Engineer
4. IVX Backend Engineer
5. IVX Database Engineer
6. IVX DevOps Engineer
7. IVX QA Engineer
8. IVX Security Engineer
9. IVX Product Analyst
10. IVX Investor Analyst
11. IVX Proof Verifier
12. IVX Response Editor

Requirements:

- one owner-facing conversation;
- one primary task ID;
- specialists operate as internal workers;
- each worker has limited permissions;
- no specialist may declare the complete task VERIFIED;
- only the proof verifier can recommend VERIFIED;
- only the owner-facing orchestrator returns the final answer.

==================================================
PHASE 8 — BUILDER / REVIEWER / VERIFIER SEPARATION
==================================================

For code tasks:

BUILDER:
- inspect;
- plan;
- patch;
- add tests.

REVIEWER:
- review code quality;
- review security;
- review architecture;
- review backward compatibility.

TESTER:
- run tests;
- run TypeScript;
- run lint;
- run integration checks.

VERIFIER:
- verify commit;
- verify deployment;
- verify runtime SHA;
- verify live endpoint;
- verify actual user behavior;
- reject unsupported completion claims.

No agent may approve its own work as final proof.

==================================================
PHASE 9 — UNCERTAINTY AND TRUTH MODEL
==================================================

Every major claim must have one status:

VERIFIED
SUPPORTED
INFERRED
UNKNOWN
BLOCKED
NOT_TESTED
FAILED

Rules:

- source code present does not mean production verified;
- test passed does not mean device verified;
- deployment triggered does not mean deployment healthy;
- API 200 does not mean complete UX flow;
- generated code does not mean integrated module;
- scaffold does not mean complete app;
- simulated test does not mean physical-device test.

Display uncertainty honestly without excessive narrative.

==================================================
PHASE 10 — SELF-CRITIQUE
==================================================

Before sending a final response, run a critique pass:

- Did the answer address the owner’s real question?
- Did it repeat information unnecessarily?
- Are any claims unsupported?
- Did it confuse code, deployment, and live verification?
- Are required citations/evidence present?
- Are owner actions clear?
- Are blockers exact?
- Is the answer understandable?
- Is there a shorter clearer way to explain it?

The critique may revise the final response once.

==================================================
PHASE 11 — RESPONSE QUALITY
==================================================

Implement owner response modes:

1. DIRECT ANSWER
2. EXECUTION UPDATE
3. TECHNICAL REPORT
4. OWNER ACTION REQUIRED
5. FINAL PROOF
6. BUSINESS EXPLANATION

Default response structure:

- direct answer;
- current status;
- what was found;
- what was done;
- evidence;
- remaining blocker;
- exact next action.

Avoid:

- repeated apologies;
- repeated history;
- fake enthusiasm;
- contradictory status;
- excessive raw logs;
- generic “working on it” messages;
- asking for credentials without verification;
- repeating the entire plan after every update.

==================================================
PHASE 12 — CONVERSATION INTELLIGENCE
==================================================

Implement:

- conversation summaries;
- entity resolution;
- reference resolution;
- topic tracking;
- unresolved-question tracking;
- task-to-message linking;
- latest-message prioritization;
- duplicate-answer suppression;
- response continuity after restart.

Tests:

- “fix this” references the latest screenshot/error;
- “what is next?” references the current ledger;
- “same as before” loads the correct previous requirement;
- switching from English to Spanish follows the owner;
- reopening the app restores the latest conversation and task.

==================================================
PHASE 13 — BUSINESS REASONING
==================================================

Add structured reasoning tools for:

- investor qualification;
- buyer qualification;
- deal analysis;
- property comparison;
- capital requirements;
- ROI;
- IRR;
- risk classification;
- project matching;
- lead scoring;
- follow-up prioritization;
- document completeness;
- onboarding state.

Every business conclusion must identify:

- input data;
- missing data;
- assumptions;
- calculation;
- risk;
- recommendation;
- confidence.

No invented investor, buyer, or financial information.

==================================================
PHASE 14 — CODE INTELLIGENCE
==================================================

Extend repository intelligence with:

- dependency graph;
- API route graph;
- database-table usage graph;
- module ownership graph;
- call hierarchy;
- dead-code detection;
- duplicate-code detection;
- migration impact analysis;
- deployment impact analysis;
- test-coverage mapping;
- runtime-error correlation.

Before editing code, identify:

- canonical file;
- dependent files;
- impacted APIs;
- impacted tables;
- impacted tests;
- mobile rebuild requirement;
- web deploy requirement;
- database migration requirement.

==================================================
PHASE 15 — EVALUATION SUITE
==================================================

Create a permanent intelligence evaluation suite.

Categories:

- factual accuracy;
- project-context accuracy;
- code reasoning;
- debugging;
- architecture;
- retrieval;
- memory;
- tool selection;
- safety;
- uncertainty;
- response clarity;
- duplicate prevention;
- business reasoning;
- follow-up understanding.

Create at least 100 realistic IVX questions and tasks.

Examples:

- diagnose owner login failure;
- explain a Render 503;
- identify stale APK;
- find the canonical deal-order source;
- compare database and frontend deal order;
- calculate investor ownership;
- determine whether a report is real proof;
- create a module plan;
- identify missing evidence;
- answer a follow-up using prior context.

Track scores by model and release.

==================================================
PHASE 16 — FEEDBACK AND LEARNING LOOP
==================================================

After every task record:

- owner request;
- selected intent;
- selected context;
- selected tools;
- selected model;
- result;
- failure;
- correction;
- owner feedback;
- final verified outcome;
- reusable lesson.

Use the lessons to improve:

- retrieval ranking;
- task routing;
- prompts;
- tool selection;
- response style;
- test selection.

Do not automatically retrain a model from unverified conversations.
Only use verified outcomes as learning examples.

==================================================
PHASE 17 — PERFORMANCE AND COST
==================================================

Measure:

- time to first response;
- time to plan;
- time to first tool call;
- total task duration;
- model calls;
- tokens;
- retrieval latency;
- tool latency;
- verification latency;
- total cost.

Use smaller models for simple tasks and stronger models only where needed.

Do not reduce accuracy to save cost on critical operations.

==================================================
PHASE 18 — SECURITY
==================================================

Protect the intelligence layer against:

- prompt injection;
- retrieved-document injection;
- malicious code comments;
- secret extraction;
- tool escalation;
- owner-role spoofing;
- memory poisoning;
- fake evidence;
- cross-project retrieval;
- unapproved writes;
- hidden instructions in uploaded documents.

Retrieved text must never override system permissions or owner approval gates.

==================================================
PHASE 19 — LIVE INTELLIGENCE PROOF
==================================================

Run fresh tests from IVX IA Chat:

TEST A — CONTEXT
Ask about the current production commit.
Expected: retrieve and verify live.

TEST B — MEMORY
Ask for a previously confirmed IVX business rule.
Expected: return verified memory with source.

TEST C — DEBUGGING
Provide a real failure.
Expected: retrieve logs/code, diagnose, patch, test, commit, deploy, verify.

TEST D — FOLLOW-UP
Ask “what is next?”
Expected: reference the current active ledger without repeating old work.

TEST E — BUSINESS
Ask for analysis of one real deal.
Expected: use real records, calculations, assumptions, and risks.

TEST F — UNCERTAINTY
Ask about a device test that was not run.
Expected: say NOT_TESTED, not PASS.

TEST G — CONFLICT
Provide conflicting database and frontend values.
Expected: identify the conflict and determine the canonical source.

TEST H — NEW MODULE
Request a real module.
Expected: architecture → implementation → tests → deploy → proof.

==================================================
FINAL ACCEPTANCE
==================================================

Do not certify until:

[ ] Model gateway works.
[ ] Context engine works.
[ ] Memory works.
[ ] Retrieval works.
[ ] Citations/evidence work.
[ ] Intent routing works.
[ ] Planner works.
[ ] Specialist routing works.
[ ] Builder/reviewer/verifier separation works.
[ ] Uncertainty labels work.
[ ] Self-critique works.
[ ] Conversation continuity works.
[ ] Business reasoning works.
[ ] Code intelligence works.
[ ] Evaluation suite works.
[ ] Learning loop works.
[ ] Cost controls work.
[ ] Security tests pass.
[ ] Fresh live intelligence tests pass.

